#### Overview

## How do you make manufacturing feel like creating?

Kedonia makes custom plush toys on one promise: everyone can be an IP creator. In a team of ten, I designed its identity, its product flow and its website.

#### Problem

## Between an idea and a plush sits a long production cycle.

A plush runs from the first sketch through production to the final delivery, and the person with the idea sees almost none of it. The job was to turn that complex cycle into a creative flow they could follow from start to finish.

#### Brand

## The brand became a mascot, not a wordmark.

My first direction was a professional, bold logo set in text. Then the obvious question: if we are a plush creative and manufacturing company, why not build an IP ourselves? So the brand turned into a search for our mascot.

![](/images/experience/kedonia/image.webp)

#### AI plush generation

## From an idea to an order in under two minutes.

Customers generate a plush with AI, iterate on it and order it in one flow, turning curiosity into conversions.

{% points %}
{% point title="Generate" text="Describe the plush and get a design." /%}

{% point title="Review" text="Iterate until it looks right." /%}

{% point title="Confirm" text="Choose size and quantity, and place the order." /%}

{% point title="Done" text="The order is in." /%}
{% /points %}

![](/images/experience/kedonia/image-1.webp)

#### Landing page

## Manufacturing sites are boring. This one should not be.

Kedonia sits between a creative studio and a manufacturer, so the site has small, delicate interactions everywhere, even in the FAQ.

